# Colloquy (the main/root thread - what actually happens across the whole installation)
# The most comprehensive timeline in this folder: it traces the real "main thread" behavior of the running process (main.py's colloquy1(), which blocks in Server2's own request loop, NOT a BaseThread) plus every thread it eventually causes to run, top to bottom. See the other thread-*.timeline files for each individual thread's own isolated behavior - this one is the merge.
#
# Important: colloquy/__init__.py's Colloquy class extends BaseThread but overrides none of setup()/loop()/setdown() (they'd raise NotImplementedError if ever run), and .start() is never called on a Colloquy instance anywhere in this codebase - Colloquy is used purely as the root container object, never as a running thread. The actual OS main thread is main.py's colloquy1(), which blocks inside Server2.run()'s `while True: httpd.handle_request()` (server2/__init__.py) - a plain synchronous loop, not a BaseThread. A human must open the web UI and start Exposition (or Hardware directly) before any of the rest of this timeline can happen.

0 py main.py runs colloquy1() (main.py): Colloquy() constructed - Base tree built, local/params.json loaded, every Hardware/Male/Female/Bar/Drive object created, no thread started
0 U2D2 (COM4) and Arduino serial ports opened; every Dynamixel servo initialized
0.5 all neopixels forced on, then off again - the Arduino-reboot LED-clear step
0 Server2 starts listening on http://localhost:8087/ and blocks in its own request-handling loop (not a BaseThread) - the process is now up, serving the web UI, but every Male/Female/Bar/Drive/Search thread is still unstarted. Nothing moves or lights up on its own yet
0 a human opens /app/exposition (or /app/hardware) in the web UI and clicks "start" - see thread-exposition.timeline
0 Exposition.setup() starts Hardware - see thread-exposition.timeline, thread-hardware.timeline
0 Hardware.setup() starts every Male, every Female, and the Bar, all at once - see thread-hardware.timeline
0 every Male/Female's setup() starts its own Drives, which starts its own O and P Drive threads - see thread-male.timeline, thread-female.timeline, thread-drives-male.timeline, thread-drives-female.timeline, thread-drive.timeline. Every drive-indicator LED across all 5 bodies begins tracking its drive value from this instant
0 Bar.setup() just initializes its own servo - it does nothing else yet (see thread-bar.timeline)
180 every drive on every body climbs toward the frustration threshold (75) at 1 increment/2.4s, unless a body's configured start value already put it above/below a threshold - in which case the next line can happen immediately instead of after this stretch (see thread-drive.timeline, and startup.timeline for the single-body version of this)
0 the first body (male or female) whose O or P drive crosses out of "satisfied": if it's a male, his Search starts (sway + blink his identity/drive pattern - see thread-search-male.timeline, thread-blink.timeline); if it's a female, her Search starts (sway + read_pattern - see thread-search-female.timeline, thread-read-pattern.timeline)
0 as soon as any male is searching, the Bar starts its own full-range search sway too - see thread-bar.timeline, thread-search-bar.timeline. This is independent of which specific male or female pair is actually relevant
0 from here the installation runs as a superposition of every other thread-*.timeline in this folder, all ticking independently and concurrently: multiple bodies can be searching or idle at once, the bar sways continuously once any male is frustrated, and any female whose search is running is continuously trying to decode whichever male's pattern her sensor happens to see (male_frustrated_to_female_read.timeline traces one specific instance of this end to end; two_males_contend_for_bar.timeline traces what happens when the single shared bar has to arbitrarily serve two frustrated males at once)
0 none of Male.search / Female.search / Bar.search have a stop condition tied to satisfaction (SCENARIOS.md 1.2, 4.1) - once a body starts searching it keeps sweeping and blinking/reading indefinitely until that body's own setdown() is called, which only happens if Hardware itself is stopped
0 stopping: a human calls /shutdown (or an unhandled exception anywhere trips Server2.wsgi()'s catch-all into colloquy.emergency_stop()) - this walks back down the same tree: Hardware.setdown() stops every Male/Female (each of which stops its own Drives; any running Search/Blink/ReadPattern stops via the children-stop mechanism, not always explicitly - see thread-female.timeline, thread-hardware.timeline), and the process either exits or re-execs itself (/restart, os.execl in Server2.restart_process())
